course: dictionary
lesson: 8935
title: Words for animals from far away
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A dictionary tells you what a word means, and a sentence tells you how it is used. Good readers check both: look at the words around the new word first, then the dictionary entry, and make sure the two fit together.
explain: «Six kinds of deer live wild in Britain: red, roe, fallow, sika, muntjac and Chinese water deer.» The list is a big clue. Sika is one kind of deer, sitting among other deer names. The sentence also says the last four were brought by people, so they are not native to Britain.
explain: «About 1,200 sika deer roam freely in Nara, Japan, as protected sacred animals.» Roam means wander about freely, and protected means people keep them safe. Try your meaning in the sentence: does «wander about freely» still make sense? Then it is probably right.
fact: A sika is a kind of deer that came from East Asia, such as Japan, China and Taiwan.
fact: A sika is small to medium-sized, with a reddish-brown coat and white spots in summer.
fact: Sika deer were brought to the UK, Ireland, New Zealand and the USA, where many now live in the wild.
fact: «Sika» can be a noun in the plural too: you can write a sika, sika or sikas, and say sika deer or a sika stag.
fact: In Nara, Japan, many sika deer have learned to bob their heads for special crackers.
exercise: Where did sika deer first come from? | Northern Canada | *East Asia, such as Japan and China | Africa | South America
exercise: «Six kinds of deer live wild in Britain» lists sika among them. Which two of these deer are native to Britain? | muntjac and Chinese water deer | *red and roe | fallow and sika | sika and muntjac
exercise: «About 1,200 sika deer roam freely in Nara, Japan.» What does roam mean here? | Stand still in one place | *Wander about freely | Sleep in a den | Run in a race
test: What does a sika's coat look like in summer? | *Reddish-brown with white spots | Plain grey all year | Golden yellow | Black with white stripes
test: Which country did people bring sika deer to? | China | Taiwan | Japan | *New Zealand
exam: In the sentence «Six kinds of deer live wild in Britain: red, roe, fallow, sika, muntjac and Chinese water deer», what does sika mean? | A kind of tree | A river in Japan | A kind of bird | *A kind of deer from East Asia
